      "latency_ms": 2668.6,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 583,
      "output": "At its simplest, **water** is the most abundant and essential molecule for life on Earth. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, looking at it through chemical, physical, and ecological lenses:\n\n### 1. The Chemical Definition\nChemically, water is a compound made of two types of atoms: **Hydrogen** and **Oxygen**. \n* Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**. \n* This means every single molecule of water is made up of **two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom**.\n\n### 2. Physical Properties\nWater is unique because it has several remarkable physical properties that make life possible:\n* **The Three States:** Water is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states: **liquid** (water), **solid** (ice), and **gas** (steam or water vapor).\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of its chemical structure (it has a slight positive charge on one side and a slight negative charge on the other), water is exceptionally good at dissolving other substances—like salt, sugar, and nutrients. This is how our bodies transport nutrients and how oceans stay salty.\n* **Density Anomaly:** Most substances shrink and get denser when they freeze. Water, however, *expands* and becomes less dense when it turns into ice. This is why ice floats. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, killing all aquatic life.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps living organisms from overheating.\n\n### 3. Water on Earth\n* **Coverage:** About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered in water, mostly by oceans and seas.\n* **Scarcity:** Of all that water, about **96.5% is saltwater** in the oceans. Only **2.5% is freshwater**, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible for human use in lakes, rivers, and soil.\n\n### 4. Importance to Life\nWater is the fundamental building block of life as we know it. \n* **Biology:** The human body is made up of roughly **60% water**. We use it to regulate body temperature, flush out waste, lubricate joints, and carry oxygen and nutrients through our blood.\n* **Ecosystems:** Plants need water to perform photosynthesis (how they make their food and produce the oxygen we breathe), and countless animals and plants live inside aquatic ecosystems.\n\nIn short, water is a simple molecule with complex, miraculous properties that make it the lifeblood of our planet."
